## Introduction
Since entering the $21^{\mathrm{st}}$ century, electronic information technology has developed rapidly and deeply implemented all aspects of daily life, production, and national defense [l-4]. At the same time, invisible electromagnetic (EM) waves are all around us all the time, affecting our physical and mental health, information, and national defense security to a certain extent [5,6]. At present, EM absorption and EM shielding are the two most efficient and safer strategies to solve EM radiation [7,8]. Therein, compared with traditional EM shielding, EM absorption has attracted wide attention from researchers because of its advantages of significantly reducing secondary EM pollution [9]. In the past few decades, the research on the absorption properties of typical solid EM waves absorbing materials has made great achievements, but there are still some shortcomings [l0], such as heavy weight, narrow frequency band, and thick thickness. Considering these shortcomings, magnetic metal/carbon composites with porous structures and uniform heterojunction are recognized as ideal candidates for EM absorption [11].
Metal-organicframeworks(MOFs)areakindof organic-inorganic hybrid material with a crystal structure, which is formed by the self-assembly of organic ligands and metal ions or clusters through coordination bonds in appropriate solutions [12-14]. Thanks to the advantages of high porosity, high specific surface area, low density, and adjustable chemical composition, these kinds of materials have become the focus of research in recent years, such as catalysis [15],adsorption [16], energy storage [17],medicine, EM waves absorption, and many other fields [18-20]. Magnetic metal/carbon composites can be obtained directly by pyrolysis of MOFs, while retaining the structure and morphology of MOFs. However, most MOFs derived absorption materials are composed of a single magnetic metal and carbon material, resulting in a relatively narrow absorption bandwidth. In addition, the destruction of microstructure and the reduction of pore structure and surface area during carbonization,which are not conducive to the final microwave absorption, are still to be overcome. In order to achieve the goal of strong absorption and wide absorption band, it is very important to reasonably design homogeneous heterostructures, and porous and petal-like lamellar structures in MOFs derived absorption materials. The petal-like lamellar structure on the material surface increases the specific surface area and the multiple reflection and scattering of electromagnetic waves. Besides, the combination of multiple heterogeneous metals can effectively adjust the synergistic effect of dielectric and magnetic loss [2l], as well as the dipole polarization and interface polarization. However, it is still a major challenge to achieve the above two modifications simultaneously through simple and effective methods. The traditional template method is not only time-consuming and complex, but also difficult to effectively control the composition [22]. Particularly, the design and regulation of micro and nanostructures are difficult to achieve with the template method. The final morphology and structure of samples can hardly be affected by the adjustment of micro/nanostructures of sacrificial templates. Meantime, in the process of removing the template, the morphology of the sample is destroyed, which made it more difficult to inherit the original morphology of MOFs. In addition, this method can not introduce new heterojunctions to enhance the electromagnetic loss of materials. Therefore, it is urgent to develop a simple and efficient strategy to prepare heterogeneous MOFs precursors with folded structures, which plays a foundational role in regulating the micro/nanostructures and heterojunction boundaries of MOFs derived absorbers.
In this paper, we propose a new synergistic strategy of deposition and replacement of heterogeneous metal ions to prepare cobalt iron based zeolite imidazole (zeolite imidazole frame-67 (ZIF-67)) polyhedron with folded surface structure, and then transform it into heterogeneous $\mathrm{CoFe@N}$ -doped carbon porous polyhedron $({\mathrm{CoFe@NCP}})$ through pyrolysis process. This method well retains the inherent structure of MOFs. Heterostructure and petal-like lamellar structure can be uniformly formed by cation replacement in $\mathrm{FeSO_{4}}$ solution, which is conducive to multiple reflection and scattering of electromagnetic waves and magnetic coupling of heterogeneous magnetic metals. The $\mathrm{CoFe@NCP}$ prepared by this method has the characteristics of large porosity, a large number of heterogeneous interfaces, and good impedance matching. In addition, this displacement process effectively prevents the collapse of the skeleton and the aggregation of magnetic particles, and retains the original structure of the polyhedron. Under the synergistic effect, CoFe@NCP exhibits an excellent reflection loss value of $-67.30$ dB at a thinner thickness of $2.88~\mathrm{mm}$ and the effective absorption bandwidth (EAB) of8.40 GHz (9.6-18 GHz).
