X-ray diffraction (XRD) was employed to confirm the crystal structure of the samples in Fig. 1(b). It can be found that the three peaks at $44.2^{\circ},51.5^{\circ}\mathrm{_{:}}$ and $75.9^{\circ}$ respectively in S0, S1, S2, and S3, representing (1ll), (200),and (220) crystal planes of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ (PDF $\#150806)$ [23].Additionally, the peaks of SO at $26.3^{\circ}$ are observed, corresponding to the (002) crystal plane of C. S2 and S3 contain two well-resolved peaks at $45.1^{\circ}$ and $83.2^{\circ},$ which indicate that there is a body-centered cubic structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ phase (JCPDS 50-0795).However, the peaks of the $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ phase can not be found in Sl, which is ascribed to the high content of $\mathrm{Co}$ and the low content of Fe. The results show that $\mathrm{Co^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ can be successfully reduced to $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ by reaction with carbon species at high temperatures in an argon atmosphere. It can also be seen that the peak intensity of $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ prominently enhanced with the increase of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ replacement degree, while the peak intensity of $\mathrm{Co}$ decreased, indicating that crystallinity was improved after the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ replacement process During the preparation of CoFe@NCP by pyrolysis precursor, carbon in organic matter undergoes rearrangement and phase transformation. As shown in Fig.l(c), two characteristic peaks of C at l,340 and $1{,}590\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be observed, which are defined as D and G peaks, respectively. The D peak comes from disordered carbon and atomic defects, while the G peak comes from the in-plane stretching vibration of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybrid carbon [24]. The graphitization degree and defects of carbon can be evaluated by the relative strength ratio of the D-band and Gband $(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ . The $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ of SO, S1, S2, and S3 is 1.13,1.05, 1.02, and 1.03, respectively. The $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ values exhibit a slight downward trend with increasing content $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ，indicating the increase of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ replacement degree promotes the graphitization degree of CoFe@NCP. The high degree of graphitization of carbon is conducive to improving conductive loss [25]. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) efficiently analyzed the surface chemical composition and functional groups of $\mathrm{CoFe@NCP}$ composites. For the high-resolution C 1s spectrum analysis (Fig.Sl in the ESM), there are two peaks at 284.8 and $285.2\mathrm{eV}$ ，corresponding to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-C/C=C}$ and C-N groups, respectively. The presence of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-C/C=C}$ bond is attributed to the pyrolysis of zeolite imidazole. The formation of C-N bonds further confirms successful N doping in carbon architectures. The related three peaks of highresolution O 1s spectrum at 531.1, 532.5, and $533.9\mathrm{eV}$ conform to $\mathrm{C=O},$ C-O, and $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups. Among them, the C-O chemical bond is caused by weakly conducting groups $(-\mathrm{OH})$ bonded with carbon atoms. From the high-resolution spectrum of N 1s (Fig. 1(d)),three peaks located at about 398.8, 399.9, and $401.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be observed, indexing to pyridine nitrogen, pyrrole nitrogen, and graphite nitrogen, respectively, indicating that nitrogen is successfully doped to graphitized carbon conductive networks (Fig. 1(e)). According to the high-resolution Fe 2p spectrum of S1, S2, and S3, the Fe 2p spectrum has three modalities Fe species: metallic $\mathrm{Fe^{0}}$ （ $709.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ _， $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ 0 $711.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ 0 $713.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ .The high-resolution $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum is shown in Fig.S3 in the ESM, the peaks at 778.5, 780.2, and $781.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to metallic ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{{\mathrm{0}}}}$ 。 ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ 。 and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ 。 respectively. XPS spectroscopy results demonstrate the successful reduction of metal ions, which were the same as those of XRD. The existence of metal ions is mainly attributed to partial oxidation in air.
For the analysis of electron microscope and nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm, the product after high temperatures pyrolysis has a hollow structure and porous micromorphology. Based on the conductive network model and charge transfer mechanism, the hollow porous structure effectively increases the conductive network of the material, thus increasing the conductive loss [26]. According to Maxwell-Garnett theory, the porous structure is conducive to impedance matching due to its adjustment in the effective permittivity. Meanwhile, these structures also introduce a large number of heterogeneous interfaces and defects, which can effectively improve interface polarization and dipole polarization [27,28]. The morphology of S0, S1, S2, and S3 are displayed in Figs. 1(j)-l(m). The SEM images clearly reveal that S0, Sl,S2,and S3 inherit the rhombic dodecahedron of ZIF-67 (Fig.S5 in the ESM). The SEM image of
S0 shows that the dodecahedron with a smooth surface has a uniform particle size of about $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ .With the increase of $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ replacement, the surface of S1 is beginning to become rough and appears the subtle folds. With the further replacement of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ the nanosheets in S2 are clearly visible with an overall petal-like lamellar structure. The appearance of the petal lamellar structure increases the non-homogeneous interface, which favors the increase of interfacial polarization. The petal-like lamellar structure of S3 nearly disappears, leaving a few fine folds, which may be due to the collapse of the layered structure caused by the increase of the pore structure. It can be seen that the surfaces of S1, S2, and S3 are rough, where the hollow structures appear inside S2 and S3 (Fig.S7 in the ESM). It can also be seen that the replacement of Fe increases the specific surface area of $\mathrm{CoFe@NCP}$ composites. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET） was used to characterize the specific surface area and porosity of the sample. As shown in Figs. 1(f)-1(i), CoFe@NCP composites show a typical type-IV isotherm, suggesting that the composites hold a porous structure [29]. The trend of sharp increase at low pressure $(P/P_{0}<0.1)$ indicated the existence of micropores, while the hysteresis loop at high pressure informed that there were a large number of media to large pores in the composite. The average pore diameter (Figs. S6(a) and S6(b) in the ESM) of CoFe@NCP composites replaced by $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ is much larger than that of SO, reaching about $13\ \mathrm{nm}.$ In addition, the BET surface area increases, which is $131.06\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for S0, $205.81\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for S1, $432.78\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for S2, and $526.65~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for S3, respectively. The larger specific surface area leads to charge accumulation, which increases the interfacial polarization.
To further insight into detailed structural information of S0, S1, S2, and S3, the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high resolution TEM (HRTEM) were employed. TEM images distinctly demonstrate the internal cavity for Sl, S2, and S3. As shown in Figs.2(b)-2(i), it is obvious that the size of the particles distributed on the carbon matrix is significantly larger compared to S0. A distinct petal-like lamelar structure can be observed on the surfaces of S1, S2, and S3. With the increase of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ replacement degree, a petal-like lamellar structure is more obvious. Moreover, we can observe that heterogeneous CoFe crystal particles of S1, S2, and S3 dispersedly distribute in the carbon matrix. The elemental mapping distribution results confirmed that the Fe, Co, and C elements are evenly distributed throughout the whole polyhedron, illustrating that heterostructure is evenly distributed in the polyhedron. HRTEM image shows that the lattice spacing of $0.202~\mathrm{nm}$ corresponds to the (1l0) lattice plane of CoFe particles and the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) image (Fig. $2(\mathrm{k}),$ also confirms the (2ll） and (ll0) lattice planes of CoFe crystal particles. Simultaneously, the HRTEM image also reveals that CoFe is encapsulated by graphitic carbon layers with an interplane spacing of $0.304\ \mathrm{nm},$ confirming the formation of uniform core-shell heterojunctions between magnetic alloy particles and dielectric graphitic carbon. Balanced impedance matching and enhanced attenuation can be achieved due to the formation of porous structures, core-shell heterojunctions, magnetic particles, and heterogeneous interfaces. The schematic diagram of structural evolution from So to S3 caused by iron ion replacement is shown in Fig.2(a).
In order to study the mechanism of microwave absorption, Fig. 3 shows the electromagnetic parameters of CoFe $\@$ NCP with a filling amount of $40\mathrm{wt.\%}$ . The real parts of permittivity $(\varepsilon^{\prime})$ and permeability $(\mu^{\prime})$ imply the energy storage and the imaginary parts 0 $\stackrel{\cdot}{\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ represent the dissipation capability for electric and magnetic energy [12,23,30]. As shown in Figs. 3(a) and 3(b), the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ of composites decreased with the increase of frequency. At $2{\mathrm{-}}18\ \mathrm{GHz},$ the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ of all samples corresponds to the changes from 6.3 to 3.7, 8.8 to 3.9, 10.0 to 4.3, and 9.2 to 4.l, respectively. The $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values of the samples also decrease accordingly from 4.3 to 1.1, 3.6 to 1.5, 5.1 to 2.5, and 9.9 to 3.l at $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz},$ respectively. The $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ of SO are the lowest, which is due to the poor conductivity of the amorphous carbon skeleton formed during the pyrolysis of ZIF-67 [27]. Due to the introduction of a heterogeneous interface, both $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values of Sl, S2, and S3 have significantly increased. With the increase in replacement degree, the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ of the $\varsigma_{x}$ increases at first and then decreases (Fig. 3(a)). Under the replacement of different $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , the polyhedron size of samples does not change much (Fig.2), and S3 has the largest specific surface area and porosity in Fig. S6 in the ESM. Therefore, S3 with cavity occupies the largest volume ratio, which makes $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ significantly higher than the other three in Fig.3(b). At the same time, the tighter the filling state, the more paths and options are provided for carrier migration [31]. Combined with the changing trend of dielectric loss in Fig.3(c), the dielectric loss capacity of S1, S2, and S3 increases with the increase of replacement degree, which can be related to interface polarization. In the process of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ replacement, CoFe alloy was introduced, and the hollow porous structure was formed at the same time. Compared with SO, more heterogeneous interfaces are formed between magnetic components and the carbon layers, which leads to the improvement of interface polarization. In addition, a hollow porous structure makes air media with different dielectric properties enter and form interface polarization with materials.
Figures 3(d) and 3(e) depict the variation of $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ of permeability values. Multiple peaks in the curve of $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ indicated a stronger response to the EM waves in $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ The maximum values of all samples are achieved at $2\ {\mathrm{GHz}},$ then decrease to a frequency of 6 GHz because of the Snoek limits (Fig. 3(d)) [32]. From 6 to 18 GHz, S0 and S3 show a similar floating trend between 1.l and 1.O with the increase of frequency, reflecting similar magnetic behavior. Coincidentally, Sl and S2 have similar magnetic behavior. The $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ of S1 is larger than the other composites in most of the frequency range, reaching a maximum value of 0.35. The corresponding $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ curves  show resonant peaks at the frequency of around $12\ \mathrm{GHz},$ which are attributed to natural ferromagnetic resonance (Fig.3(e)） [33]. Figure 3(f) shows that $\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\mu}$ of the magnetic loss angle of all materials is much lower than that of the dielectric loss $\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\varepsilon},$ which indicates that the main loss mechanism of absorbing EM waves of the composite is the dielectric loss.
According to Debye theory, dielectric loss possesses polarization and conductance loss. The Cole-Cole curve $(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}–\varepsilon^{\prime})$ can be utilized to study the polarization process. The correlation between $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ can be expressed by Eq. (5)
$$
\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
where $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}$ and $\varepsilon_{\infty}$ are the static permittivity and relative permittivity in higher frequency regions, respectively. Each semicircle on Cole-Cole curves corresponds to the relaxation process [34]. Figure $3(\mathrm{g)}$ reveals the Cole-Cole plots of CoFe@NCP composites in the frequency range of $2{\mathrm{-}}18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ Obviously, CoFe@NCP has more semicircles, which means that the replacement process introduces more relaxation processes. There were abundant heterointerfaces between metal nanoparticles and carbon shells in these heterogeneous composites. Thus, the formation of interfacial polarization was due to the charge accumulation at the interfaces under alternating electromagnetic fields. It is not difficult to see that the Debye circle of the sample is irregular, which means that in addition to the relaxation process, there are other dielectric loss mechanisms in the composites, such as electron polarization, dipole polarization, and other polarization. Thus, under the condition of a weak magnetic field and GHz band, the magnetic loss of magnetic materials mainly comes from eddy current loss, natural resonance, and exchange resonance [35,36]. The relationship between eddy current coefficient $C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{1}$ and frequency can be used to analyze whether eddy current loss contributes to magnetic loss [37]. Magnetic loss is essential due to the presence of magnetic metal in the prepared composites, and saturation magnetization is one of the indexes to evaluate the magnetic loss [38]. It is obvious that the kind and content of metal particles, and the microstructure of composites all determine the saturation magnetization of composites [6]. As the replacement degree of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ increases, the magnetic saturation $(M_{s})$ of $\mathrm{CoFe@NCP}$ composites decreases. The $M_{s}$ of S1, S2, and S3 with hollow heterostructures decreased compared to that of solid S0. As shown in Fig.S8 in the ESM, the $M_{s}$ values of samples are 340.11, 106.87, 90.86, and $96.35\mathrm{\emu/g},$ respectively. The coercivity $\left(H_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ of S0, S1, S2, and S3 samples is 412.4l,189.24, 461.96, and $378.34\mathrm{Oe},$ respectively. If $C_{0}$ changes little or is almost unchanged with the increase of frequency, the magnetic loss mainly comes from eddy current loss [39]. Obviously, the $C_{0}$ values of S1, S2, and S3 barely change from 8 to $10\ \mathrm{GHz},$ indicating that there is eddy current loss. What's more, the peaks of all samples at about 5 GHz belong to natural resonance,and the peaks of Sl and S2 near l2 GHz come from exchange resonance. Typically, attenuation constants $(\alpha)$ are used to evaluate the attenuation capability of the absorber to electromagnetic waves. The value of the $\alpha$ can be expressed by Eq. (6) [8,40]
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}+\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}
$$
The $\alpha$ values of the samples increased over the entire frequency range in Fig.3(i). Compared with S0, the introduction of $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ significantly increases $\alpha$ of S1, S2, and S3, further indicating the hollow porous structure formed by replacement is easier to form a conductive network, which is conducive to the absorption of electromagnetic waves.
The EM absorbing performance results of all samples at $40\%$ weight are shown in the Fig.4. Reflection loss (RL) is the most direct and effective criterion to reflect the microwave absorption properties of materials [4l]. Generally, the transmission-line theory is applied to calculate the RL of materials, and the calculation equations can appear as follows [42, 43]
$$
Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_{0}{\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{\varepsilon}}}\mathrm{tanh}\left[\mathrm{j}\left({\frac{2f\pi t}{c}}\right){\sqrt{\mu\varepsilon}}\right]
$$
$$
{\mathrm{RL}}=20\mathrm{log}\left|{\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_{0}}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_{0}}}\right|
$$
where the impedance of freedom space is represented by $Z_{\mathrm{in}},$ the input impedance is denoted by $Z_{0},\ c$ is the speed of light $(3\mathrm{~\times~}10^{8}\mathrm{~m/s})$ _； $t$ is the thickness of the absorber, and $f$ is the frequency of incident EM. In general, RL exceeding $-10$ dB means that $90\%$ of the incident EM waves can be dissipated, corresponding frequency defined as the EAB [44]. We can see that the minimum RL reaches $-23.49$ dB at $5.00\ \mathrm{mm}$ and the widest effective absorption bandwidth is 3.68 GHz for S0 with a thickness of $3.52\ \mathrm{mm}$ in Fig.4(a) and Fig.S9(a) in the ESM. Obviously, compared with the EM absorbing performance of S0, the EM absorbing performance of S1, S2, and S3 is significantly improved due to the addition of Fe particles and the existence of a hollow structure. As shown in Fig.4(b) and Fig.S9(b) in the ESM, at a thickness of $2.88~\mathrm{mm}$ for S1, $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}$ is as high as $-67.30$ dB, and EAB reaches $8.40\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (9.60-18.00 GHz). S2 possesses the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}$ $\left(-64.63~\mathrm{dB}\right)$ at $2.17~\mathrm{mm},$ and EAB covers 8.64 GHz (9.36-18.00 GHz）in Fig. $4(\mathsf{c})$ and Fig. ${\sf S9(c)}$ in the ESM. The $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}$ of S3 at thin thickness $(2.10\ \mathrm{mm})$ is as high as $-56.86$ dB,and EAB includes $7.36\mathrm{GHz}\ (10.64\mathrm{-}18.00\mathrm{GHz})$ (Fig.4(d) and Fig. S9(d) in the ESM). It is noteworthy that as the thickness increases, $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}$ shifts to lower frequencies. These results can be explained using the quarter-wavelength model $(d=n c/(4f_{\mathrm{m}}\sqrt{|\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathrm{r}}||\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{\mathrm{r}}|}))$ . Besides, the minimum RL shifts to a higher frequency and thinner thickness with an increasing degree of replacement, and the widest
EAB covers most of the X band $(8\mathrm{-}12~\mathrm{GHz})$ and the whole Ku band (12-18 GHz). Impedance matching plays a decisive role in microwaveabsorptionperformance,whichisusually characterized by $\left|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\right|$ . In fact, if $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert$ is close to l and the absorber has optimal impedance matching, most of the incident electromagnetic waves can enter the absorber and be lost [45,46]. As shown in Fig.4(e), the strong microwave absorption of Sl is due to the fact that the $\left|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\right|$ value is close to l, indicating good matching impedance performance.
The specific RL $\mathrm{(SRL~=~\vertRL\vert}$ (packing load $\times$ matching thickness)) of the absorber can directly evaluate the absorption performance of electromagnetic wave absorbing materials. The higher the SRL value is, the better the microwave absorption property of the material is. Compared with other magnetic/carbon matrix composites, it is obvious that CoFe@NCP synthesized in Fig. 4(f) has higher SRL values, and the corresponding microwave absorption properties are listed in Table l.
A perfect electric conductor (PEC) substrate model covered by absorbing materials was established. With the $z$ -axis of the model as the incident direction of the EM waves, $\theta$ was defined as the detection angle. Referring to previous reports [56], the frequency corresponding to the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{min}}$ of the optimized Sl was selected for the simulation process. The three-dimensional (3D) simulation scenes of PEC covered by S0, Sl, S2, and S3 are respectively revealed in Fig.4(g). Apparently, the plane scattering signal of PEC is the strongest, and the model covered by S1 has the weakest scattering signal, which indicates that it can consume more electromagnetic energy. The graph of radar cross section (RCS) fluctuation was drawn with $\theta$ value in the range of $-60^{\circ}$ to ${60}^{\circ}$ as shown in Fig. 5(a). The RCS value can be expressed as [57, 58]
$$
\sigma(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{B}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{2})=10\mathrm{lg}\big(\big(4\pi S/\lambda^{2}\big)\big|E_{\mathrm{s}}/E_{\mathrm{i}}\big|\big)^{2}
$$
Herein, $E_{\mathrm{s}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{i}}$ signifies the electric field intensity of the scattered waves and the incident waves, respectively, and S stands for the area of the target model. When Sl with a thickness of 2.88 mm is covered, the RCS can be reduced to $26.5\ \mathrm{dB}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{2}$ and the
RCS value is completely lower than $-15\ \mathrm{dB}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{2}$ in the range of $-60^{\circ}$ to ${60}^{\circ}$ which signified high attenuation performance of radar waves. The RCS simulation images of PEC, S0, S1, S2, and S3 with large detection angles from $-180^{\circ}$ to $180^{\circ}$ are provided in Fig. 5(c). When the angle changes within $-90^{\circ}$ to $90^{\circ}$ it implies that the EM waves are the first incident into the absorbing materials. But when the $\theta$ is $-180^{\circ}<\theta<-90^{\circ}$ or $90^{\circ}<\theta<180^{\circ},$ it means that the EM waves are directly incident on the PEC substrate, thus the RCS values fluctuate little compared with PEC. Furthermore, the minimum RCS values of S0, S1, S2, and S3 achieve -53.0, -37.5, $-43.5.$ and $-50.3\ \mathrm{dB}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{2}$ respectively in Fig. 5(b). The superior RCS reduction property of Sl confirms its enormous potential in practical application.
Figure 5(d) shows the relevant electromagnetic wave attenuation mechanism of CoFe@NCP composites in detail. Firstly, when the incident wave reaches the absorber surface, due to the petal-like layered structure, internal cavity, and porous structure of CoFe@NCP, multiple reflections and scattering occur within and between CoFe@NCP surfaces. The formation of a porous  structure improves the dielectric properties  and coordinates the impedance matching of CoFe@NCP. Secondly, the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ replacement process and N doping increase the internal defects of composites, thus enhancing defect polarization. In addition, the replacement of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ introduces new CoFe heterostructures and petal-like layered structures, which makes the heterogeneous interfacial polarization of CoFe@NCP increase significantly. Therefore, the dielectric loss of the material is greatly increased. Thirdly, owing to the presence of magnetic particles, magnetic losses including eddy currents and natural resonances are introduced. Due to the synergy of the various losses mentioned above, CoFe@NCP has excellent EM wave absorption performance.
## 3 Conclusions
In this work, CoFe@NCP composites with petal-like lamellar heterostructure and hollow structure were successfully prepared by $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ replacement and deposition. The petal-like lamellar structure on the surface layer and internal pore structure of polyhedrons of $\mathrm{CoFe@NCP}$ composites can be controlled by regulating the degree of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ replacement. Fe and cobalt form iron cobalt heterojunction to enhance the dielectric loss, which plays a synergistic role in the dielectric loss and magnetic loss. The petallike lamellar structure increases the travel of EM waves in CoFe@NCP composites, and the porous structure enhances the impedancematching of CoFe@NCPcomposites.Good impedance matching characteristics and strong attenuation capability enable Sl with a strong reflection loss of $-67.30~\mathrm{dB}$ and broad EAB of $8.40\mathrm{GHz}$ It is believed that this work guides MOFsderived hollow porous nanomaterials with heterostructures with ultra-wideband absorption and high reflection loss.
